        What is attached to the prompt is listed above the text input, between
        the tabs and the input, one row per attachment in the order they were
        attached, and only while there is anything attached. An attached piece
        of text shows a quote icon, the text's first line cut off with an
        ellipsis, and, when it runs to more than one line, how many lines it
        has, muted, and at the right end a small button with a cross, whose
        tooltip says it removes the attachment, which removes just that one.

        Images can be attached too: pasted into the text input from the
        clipboard, dropped onto the chat input from the file manager, or
        chosen with a small ghost button with an image icon, whose tooltip says
        "Attach images", on the tab row between the last tab and the help
        text, which opens the platform's file picker for any files, several at
        once, since the picker can't be limited to images. Pasting an image
        attaches it rather than putting anything in the text, even when the
        clipboard holds text as well. An image is known by its content, not
        its name, and only PNG, JPEG, GIF, and WebP are taken: anything else
        pasted, dropped, or chosen, BMP, TIFF, and SVG included, is not
        attached, and a warning notification titled "Not attached" says it
        isn't an image. An image larger than 5 MB is not attached either, and
        the same notification says it is too large. While files are dragged
        over the chat input, it is highlighted. An attached
        image's row shows a small thumbnail, its file name, or "Pasted image"
        for one pasted, and its size in pixels, muted, with the same remove
        button; hovering the thumbnail shows the image larger, at most 320
        pixels. Images and text are listed together in the order they were
        attached while the prompt is written. A prompt taken back into the
        input, as when a queued prompt is edited, lists its text first, then
        its images, each image named by its saved file, a pasted one
        included.

        Attachments belong to the prompt being written, not to a tab: switching
        tabs keeps them all, and they go with the prompt whichever tab it is
        sent from. When the prompt is sent, its attachments are sent along with
        it and the list empties, as the text input does. A queued prompt keeps
        the attachments it was sent with.

    /// Attaches `text` to the prompt, after anything already attached.
    pub fn attach_text(&mut self, text: String, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        self.attach(Attaching::Text(text), cx);
    }

    /// Attaches `image` to the prompt, after anything already attached.
    pub fn attach_image(&mut self, image: AttachedImage, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        self.attach(Attaching::Image(image), cx);
    }

    fn attach(&mut self, attached: Attaching, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        self.next_attachment_id += 1;
        self.attachments.push(Attachment {
            id: self.next_attachment_id,
            attached,
        });
        cx.notify();
    }

    /// Attaches the image files at `paths`, dropped onto the chat input or
    /// chosen with its Attach images button, in order. Each that isn't an
    /// image, or is too large, is left out, a notification saying why.
    pub fn attach_paths(
        &mut self,
        paths: &[std::path::PathBuf],
        window: &mut Window,
        cx: &mut Context<Self>,
    ) {
        for path in paths {
            match AttachedImage::load(path) {
                Ok(image) => self.attach_image(image, cx),
                Err(rejected) => Self::not_attached(&rejected, window, cx),
            }
        }
    }

    /// Attaches the images on the clipboard, pasted into the text input;
    /// whether there were any, in which case nothing is put in the text.
    fn paste_images(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) -> bool {
        let Some(item) = cx.read_from_clipboard() else {
            return false;
        };
        let images: Vec<Image> = item
            .entries()
            .iter()
            .filter_map(|entry| match entry {
                ClipboardEntry::Image(image) => Some(image.clone()),
                _ => None,
            })
            .collect();
        for image in &images {
            match AttachedImage::pasted(image) {
                Ok(image) => self.attach_image(image, cx),
                Err(rejected) => Self::not_attached(&rejected, window, cx),
            }
        }
        !images.is_empty()
    }

    /// Says why an image wasn't attached.
    fn not_attached(rejected: &attached_image::Rejected, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut App) {
        window.push_notification(
            Notification::warning(rejected.message()).title("Not attached"),
            cx,
        );
    }

    /// Opens the platform's file picker for images to attach, several at
    /// once, and attaches those chosen.
    fn pick_images(&mut self, window: &mut Window, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        let picked = cx.prompt_for_paths(PathPromptOptions {
            files: true,
            directories: false,
            multiple: true,
            prompt: Some("Attach".into()),
        });
        cx.spawn_in(window, async move |this, cx| {
            let Ok(Ok(Some(paths))) = picked.await else {
                return;
            };
            this.update_in(cx, |this, window, cx| this.attach_paths(&paths, window, cx))
                .ok();
        })
        .detach();
    }

    /// Removes the attachment `id`.
    pub fn remove_attachment(&mut self, id: usize, cx: &mut Context<Self>) {
        self.attachments.retain(|attachment| attachment.id != id);
        cx.notify();
    }
